# word-break-anchor: pins where `word break` holds, and that it agrees with
# the `word` element and chunk (chapter 4, Elements and Matches; ADR 0011).
# In "cat, dog" it holds before Character 1, between "," and the space, between
# the space and "d", and at the end, so the Match Search gives four empty
# Matches, whose ranges are p..p-1. It never holds between "t" and ",", since
# neither is White_Space. Each empty match where the last one ended is
# skipped, and the search goes on one Character later. Fuel, allocation and
# state figures are estimates.
# Unblessed: the output lines are written by hand, not by bless.

> load breaks
> deliver d1 to=breaks message=go
> pump clock=2026-09-30T09:00:00Z
seg breaks/r1 start delivery=d1 handler=go clause=1 fuel=318 alloc=392 state=120 end=return
run breaks/r1 outcome=completed delivery=d1 handler=go fuel=318 alloc=392
pumped state=idle fuel=318

> vars
vars breaks breaks=[1..0, 5..4, 6..5, 9..8] wordTexts=["cat,", "dog"] tBreak=false commaBreak=true
